# Copy to backup.env and fill in. Uploaded to the droplet by
# deploy/digitalocean/deploy.sh and read by the backup container via
# env_file. Never commit the filled-in copy.

# The operator's Ed25519 signing seed, 64 hex characters. Signs the
# service manifest, every terms document, and every erasure receipt.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# **Back this up out of band, and understand what losing it costs.**
# It is not like the moderation seeds, where a loss invalidates future
# signatures and nothing else. A client that consents to this operator
# pins these exact manifest bytes, including the public key derived
# from this seed. Every retained snapshot pins a terms document signed
# with it, and every erasure receipt a holder holds verifies against
# it.
#
# Regenerate it and the operator becomes, to every enrolled client, a
# different operator wearing the same hostname: consent no longer
# matches, receipts no longer verify, and there is no route that
# repairs it — reassigning a snapshot's holder is exactly the capability
# this seat is built not to have.
BACKUP_SIGNING_SEED=

# Paid mode, and deliberately empty. With no issuers the operator never
# returns 402 and never consults an entitlement — the self-hosting path
# the profile requires, and the same binary rather than a build flavour.
#
# Nothing issues a SeatEntitlement yet, so setting these would make the
# operator refuse every upload with a refusal no client can clear. When
# a broker exists, all three are required together: the operator
# refuses to boot with issuers set and no revocation URL (a refunded
# entitlement would work until it expired) or with no offers (a 402
# naming no offer tells a client to buy something without saying what).
# BACKUP_ENTITLEMENT_ISSUERS=onym:key:<broker-hex>
# BACKUP_REVOCATION_URL=https://<broker>/v1/revocations/current
# BACKUP_OFFERS=backup-monthly-v1
